WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.873
A prepaid card has 100 on it.

2
00:00:04.873 --> 00:00:09.369
At the same moment, it pays 30 for a coffee and 50 for fuel.

3
00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:11.221
So 20 should be left.

4
00:00:11.221 --> 00:00:12.507
The card says 50.

5
00:00:12.507 --> 00:00:15.456
Somewhere, 30 just walked out the door.

6
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:21.743
And the program that keeps the balance runs one command at a time, so nothing ever runs at once.

7
00:00:21.743 --> 00:00:23.317
How did money go missing?

8
00:00:23.317 --> 00:00:24.701
And how do we stop it?

9
00:00:24.967 --> 00:00:26.970
Picture a café with one barista.

10
00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.977
She makes one drink at a time, and never starts the next until it's handed over.

11
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:38.013
Redis, a program that keeps data in memory and answers short commands, works the same way.

12
00:00:38.013 --> 00:00:43.727
One thread, a single line of work, runs every command to the end before it takes the next.

13
00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:47.313
Redis keeps each value under a name, called its key.

14
00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:50.340
The card's balance sits under the key balance:7.

15
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:56.771
Big apps run several copies of their program, so payments keep flowing if one machine fails.

16
00:00:56.771 --> 00:00:58.180
Call each copy a pod.

17
00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.735
The coffee reaches pod A, and the fuel reaches pod B.

18
00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:03.637
Each pod does what looks right.

19
00:01:03.637 --> 00:01:07.045
GET the balance, check that it's enough, then SET the new one.

20
00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:10.142
Pod A reads the balance: 100.

21
00:01:10.142 --> 00:01:12.467
Pod B reads it too: 100.

22
00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:16.549
Pod A works out 100 minus 30, and SETs 70.

23
00:01:16.549 --> 00:01:20.332
Pod B works out 100 minus 50, and SETs 50.

24
00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:23.424
The card paid 80, and the balance says 50.

25
00:01:23.424 --> 00:01:26.379
The last SET won, and it never saw the first.

26
00:01:26.379 --> 00:01:28.217
That's called a lost update.

27
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:30.461
Notice, every command ran alone.

28
00:01:30.461 --> 00:01:32.269
The thread did nothing wrong.

29
00:01:32.269 --> 00:01:37.255
A decision took two commands, and between them, the thread served the other pod.

30
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.941
A real barista would notice someone asking about your card in the middle of your order.

31
00:01:42.941 --> 00:01:45.318
Redis doesn't remember who asked what.

32
00:01:45.318 --> 00:01:47.632
It serves each command, and moves on.

33
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.299
Wouldn't it be nice to hand over the whole thing in one go?

34
00:01:51.299 --> 00:01:55.273
Redis has a batch for that, like one slip with all your orders on it.

35
00:01:55.533 --> 00:01:57.225
MULTI says: start a slip.

36
00:01:57.225 --> 00:02:01.082
Every command after it answers QUEUED instead of running.

37
00:02:01.082 --> 00:02:03.924
EXEC then runs the whole slip as one step.

38
00:02:03.924 --> 00:02:07.037
No other pod's command can land in the middle.

39
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.711
So let's put the GET on the slip too.

40
00:02:09.711 --> 00:02:11.014
What does it answer?

41
00:02:11.014 --> 00:02:12.056
Pause and guess.

42
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:15.753
QUEUED.

43
00:02:15.753 --> 00:02:19.768
The balance comes back with all the other answers, after EXEC.

44
00:02:19.768 --> 00:02:22.228
That's too late to decide what to SET.

45
00:02:22.228 --> 00:02:25.012
A batch runs together, but it can't decide.

46
00:02:25.233 --> 00:02:26.643
And one more surprise.

47
00:02:26.643 --> 00:02:32.088
If a command on the slip fails while it runs, it fails alone, and the rest still run.

48
00:02:32.088 --> 00:02:33.369
There's no rollback.

49
00:02:33.369 --> 00:02:35.867
A batch means nothing can interrupt it.

50
00:02:35.867 --> 00:02:37.853
It doesn't mean all or nothing.

51
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.070
So the decision stays in your code.

52
00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:44.065
We just need to know if anyone changed the card while we were thinking.

53
00:02:44.333 --> 00:02:45.515
Back to the café.

54
00:02:45.515 --> 00:02:49.058
You tell the barista: I'm keeping an eye on card 7.

55
00:02:49.058 --> 00:02:53.505
If anyone changes it before my slip arrives, throw my slip away.

56
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:57.078
She keeps a little list of who is watching which card.

57
00:02:57.078 --> 00:03:02.280
Whenever anyone writes card 7, she puts a mark next to every other name watching it.

58
00:03:02.533 --> 00:03:04.608
That's exactly what Redis does.

59
00:03:04.608 --> 00:03:06.013
The command is WATCH.

60
00:03:06.013 --> 00:03:09.627
The list lives inside Redis, one entry per connection.

61
00:03:09.627 --> 00:03:12.907
And a name with a mark is what Redis calls dirty.

62
00:03:13.167 --> 00:03:15.004
Let's replay the race.

63
00:03:15.004 --> 00:03:18.679
Both pods WATCH balance:7, then GET it: 100.

64
00:03:18.679 --> 00:03:21.100
Each decides in its own code.

65
00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.689
Pod A plans 70, pod B plans 50.

66
00:03:23.933 --> 00:03:25.346
Pod B is quicker.

67
00:03:25.346 --> 00:03:27.009
MULTI, SET 50, EXEC.

68
00:03:27.009 --> 00:03:30.250
Its name has no mark, so the slip runs.

69
00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:31.747
The balance is 50.

70
00:03:31.933 --> 00:03:36.116
And because the card was written, Redis marks every other watcher.

71
00:03:36.116 --> 00:03:39.285
Pod A is now dirty, and pod A doesn't know it yet.

72
00:03:39.533 --> 00:03:42.999
Now pod A sends its slip: MULTI, SET 70, EXEC.

73
00:03:42.999 --> 00:03:47.143
Without the watch, this SET would wipe out B's payment.

74
00:03:47.143 --> 00:03:48.424
What happens now?

75
00:03:48.424 --> 00:03:49.629
Pause and guess.

76
00:03:52.833 --> 00:03:54.740
EXEC checks the mark first.

77
00:03:54.740 --> 00:04:00.176
It finds it, throws the SET away, and answers nil, Redis's word for no value.

78
00:04:00.176 --> 00:04:02.859
Nothing ran, and B's payment survives.

79
00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:06.028
Nil means: you lost the race, start over.

80
00:04:06.028 --> 00:04:11.170
Pod A watches again, reads 50, checks that 30 still fits, and writes 20.

81
00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:14.597
Nobody touched the card this time, so EXEC runs.

82
00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:15.129
20.

83
00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:16.654
Both payments count.

84
00:04:16.654 --> 00:04:19.781
And notice, Redis never made anyone wait.

85
00:04:19.781 --> 00:04:23.289
It only refused a decision made from old news.

86
00:04:23.567 --> 00:04:25.926
This pattern is called check and set.

87
00:04:25.926 --> 00:04:31.985
Read, decide, and write only if nothing changed since the read, inside a loop that tries again.

88
00:04:32.233 --> 00:04:37.573
It's optimistic: it bets that clashes are rare, and pays only when one happens.

89
00:04:37.867 --> 00:04:39.641
Two things keep it honest.

90
00:04:39.641 --> 00:04:45.440
First, a watch belongs to a connection, the open line between your program and Redis.

91
00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.852
WATCH, MULTI and EXEC must travel on the same one.

92
00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:52.183
Second, every retry costs trips there and back.

93
00:04:52.183 --> 00:04:56.906
If fifty pods write the same card all day, it turns into musical chairs.

94
00:04:56.906 --> 00:05:00.448
Most attempts lose, and each loss costs another round.

95
00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:07.102
For a key that busy, send the whole decision to Redis as a script, which never needs to retry.

96
00:05:07.102 --> 00:05:07.915
That's next.

97
00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:09.743
So where did the 30 go?

98
00:05:09.743 --> 00:05:12.023
Into the gap between two commands.

99
00:05:12.023 --> 00:05:14.236
The thread was never the problem.

100
00:05:14.533 --> 00:05:16.939
A batch runs together, but can't decide.

101
00:05:16.939 --> 00:05:22.653
WATCH lets your code decide, and refuses the write if the card changed while you were thinking.

102
00:05:23.233 --> 00:05:24.562
One quick check.

103
00:05:24.562 --> 00:05:30.792
After pod A's WATCH, pod B sets balance:7 to 100, the value it already had.

104
00:05:30.792 --> 00:05:32.619
Does pod A's EXEC run?

105
00:05:32.619 --> 00:05:33.948
Pause and think.

106
00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:37.363
No.

107
00:05:37.363 --> 00:05:38.509
It answers nil.

108
00:05:38.509 --> 00:05:40.725
Redis doesn't compare values.

109
00:05:40.725 --> 00:05:45.309
Any write marks the watchers, even one that changes nothing.
